#
# WHAT IS SANDWORM?
#
# Sandworm creates a batteries-included containerized workspace where LLM
# coding agents (Claude Code, OpenAI Codex, Google Gemini CLI) can work
# freely without touching your actual system. Everything lives in .sandworm/
# — delete it, and it's like nothing ever happened.
#
# Think of it as a comfortable, fleshed-out home for AI agents to work in:
# • Full Debian userland with dev tools (gcc, make, git, Python, etc.)
# • Node.js 22, Rust stable, and npm globals pre-configured
# • Nix-portable for reproducible builds (optional)
# • JuNest for Arch Linux packages via pacman (optional)
# • OAuth "just works" — localhost callbacks and browser popups supported
# • SSH agent forwarding — git clone with your keys, no setup needed
# • GPU passthrough — NVIDIA, AMD, Intel for ML inference (opt-in)
# • Audio support — PulseAudio/PipeWire for multimedia apps
#
# WHO IS THIS FOR?
#
# • Developers who want to try LLM coding agents without installing random
#   packages globally or giving AI full access to their home directory
# • Teams that need consistent, reproducible environments across machines
# • Anyone who's thought "what did I just let this AI install on my system?"
# • People who value the ability to `rm -rf .sandworm` and start fresh
# • Vibecoders <3
#
# WHAT PROBLEMS DOES IT SOLVE?
#
# 1. DEPENDENCY HELL — All tools live in .sandworm/toolroots/, not your system
# 2. CREDENTIAL HYGIENE — API keys stay in .sandworm/home/.config/
# 3. REPRODUCIBILITY — "Works on my machine" → "Works in sandworm"
# 4. CLEANUP — Delete .sandworm/ and everything is gone. No leftovers.
# 5. AGENT AUTONOMY — LLMs get a real Linux environment they can explore
# 6. HOST INTEGRATION — SSH keys, git config, display, audio "just work"
#
# SECURITY POSTURE
#
# This is NOT a high-assurance security sandbox. It's a pragmatic improvement
# over running powerful CLI tools directly on your system:
#
# • The agent CAN read/write your workspace (that's the point)
# • The agent CAN access the network (for OAuth, APIs, downloads)
# • The agent CANNOT see .sandworm/ contents (control plane is masked)
# • The agent CANNOT mutate the host system (in SAFE mode)
#
# SAFE mode (default):
#   - Rootless container, container root (uid 0) maps to your host user
#   - Control plane masked via read-only tmpfs overlay
#   - No access to host filesystem outside /workspace
#   - Seccomp UNCONFINED by default (enables proot/JuNest/nix-portable)
#   - Host network namespace shared (enables OAuth localhost callbacks)
#   - Desktop integration enabled (X11, Wayland, audio, SSH agent)
#
# STRICT mode (--strict flag):
#   - All SAFE restrictions PLUS:
#   - Seccomp CONFINED (may break proot/JuNest/nix-portable)
#   - No desktop integration (no X11, audio, SSH agent sockets)
#   - No GPU passthrough
#   - Network tools (ip, ss, netstat) blocked by seccomp profile
#   - Host network namespace still shared (required for OAuth callbacks)
#
# UNSAFE mode (--unsafe flag, requires sudo):
#   - Host root filesystem mounted at /host (read-write)
#   - Control plane visible at /control (read-write)
#   - Can execute commands in host namespace via `host` wrapper
#   - Use only for system administration tasks
#
# If you need stronger isolation than STRICT provides, use VMs. Sandworm
# optimizes for ergonomics and "it just works" over paranoid security.
#
# KNOWN LIMITATIONS
#
# 1. Metadata leak via /proc/self/mountinfo
#    The kernel exposes overlay mount paths including the host username and
#    .sandworm directory location (e.g., /home/user/project/.sandworm/...).
#    This is inherent to how overlayfs works and cannot be masked without
#    breaking other functionality. It's information disclosure, not an escape.
#
# 2. Host identity exposed via bind mounts
#    For ergonomics, several host files are bind-mounted read-only:
#    - XAUTHORITY: exposes host home path (e.g., /home/user/.Xauthority)
#    - .gitconfig: exposes host git user.name and user.email
#    These enable X11 apps and git to "just work" without manual setup.
#    The paths are already leaked via mountinfo anyway (see #1).
#
# 3. Host network namespace shared (including STRICT)
#    Host networking is used because OAuth localhost callbacks (e.g.,
#    browser → localhost:1455 → container) must work reliably for non-
#    technical users. Alternatives like slirp4netns require port publishing
#    that frequently fails in practice. Implications:
#    - Agent CAN connect to host services (SSH, databases, web apps, etc.)
#    - Agent CANNOT bind privileged ports (<1024) or modify routes
#    - Agent CAN see host network interfaces via `ip addr` (in SAFE mode)
#
# 4. Seccomp blocks network tools in STRICT
#    The default seccomp profile blocks AF_NETLINK sockets, so `ip`, `ss`,
#    and `netstat` fail with EPERM. Network access itself is unaffected.
#    Use SAFE mode if you need network diagnostic tools.
#
# 5. Desktop integration sockets accessible (SAFE mode)
#    When SW_HOST_INTEGRATIONS=1 (default), these host sockets are exposed:
#    - D-Bus session bus (/run/user/$UID/bus)
#    - PulseAudio/PipeWire audio sockets
#    - X11/Wayland display sockets
#    This enables GUI apps, audio, and desktop notifications. Use --strict
#    or SW_HOST_INTEGRATIONS=0 to disable if not needed.
#
# QUICK START
#
#   $ ./sandworm              # First run: installs everything (~5-10 min)
#   $ ./sandworm codex        # Launch OpenAI Codex CLI
#   $ ./sandworm claude       # Launch Claude Code CLI
#   $ ./sandworm gemini       # Launch Gemini CLI
#   $ ./sandworm shell        # Explore what the agent sees
#   $ ./sandworm doctor       # Diagnose issues
#
# FILESYSTEM LAYOUT
#
#   .sandworm/                 Control plane (masked from agent view)
#   ├── state.json             Single source of truth
#   ├── home/                  Persistent HOME for the sandboxed user
#   ├── toolroots/             Immutable tool snapshots (Node, Rust, CLIs)
#   │   └── 20241223-120000-a1b2/  Versioned toolroot directory
#   ├── podman/                Project-local rootless Podman storage
#   │   ├── storage/           Container images and layers
#   │   └── xdg/               XDG runtime directory for Podman
#   ├── cache/                 Cached apt lists and downloads
#   └── logs/                  Timestamped session logs
#
# Inside the container:
#   /workspace                 Your project files (bind-mounted read-write)
#   /workspace/.sandworm       MASKED (appears empty via tmpfs overlay)
#   /home/sandworm             Persistent home directory (survives restarts)
#   /opt/llm                   Curated tools (read-only bind mount)
#   /opt/llm/bin               CLI executables (codex, claude, gemini, etc.)
#
# In UNSAFE mode additionally:
#   /host                      Host root filesystem (read-write)
#   /control                   Control plane (.sandworm/ contents, read-write)
#
# REQUIREMENTS
#
# • Linux host (kernel 5.0+ recommended for rootless containers)
# • Rootless Podman >= 4.0 installed (or bubblewrap as fallback)
# • ~3GB disk space for seed image + toolroots
# • User namespace support (check: unshare -Ur echo ok)
#
# For more information: https://h0p3.nekoweb.org/#sandworm
# SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT

